# Learning path, glossary, quiz, videos, digest issues.

CHAPTERS = [
 {"n":1,"slug":"step-1","title":"What Is a Blockchain?","read":"7 min","level":"Beginner",
  "desc":"Blockchain explained in plain English: blocks, hashes, nodes, consensus and why nobody can quietly rewrite the ledger.",
  "takeaways":["A blockchain is a shared ledger copied across thousands of computers (nodes).","Transactions are grouped into blocks; each block carries the fingerprint (hash) of the previous one.","Changing an old block breaks every hash after it — that's what makes history tamper-evident.","Consensus rules (Proof of Work, Proof of Stake) decide who adds the next block.","Public blockchains are permissionless: anyone can read, transact or run a node.","The ledger is transparent; identities are pseudonymous addresses.","Blockchains trade speed for trust-minimisation — that's a feature, not a bug."],
  "sections":[
   ["The one-sentence version","A blockchain is a database that many strangers maintain together, where every entry is linked to the one before it so the past cannot be silently edited. Banks keep one private ledger; a blockchain keeps thousands of identical public copies and uses math, not managers, to keep them in sync."],
   ["Blocks, hashes and the chain","Every few seconds or minutes, pending transactions are bundled into a block. The block is run through a hash function that produces a unique fingerprint. The next block stores that fingerprint. Change one character in an old transaction and its hash changes, which changes the next block's hash, and so on — the whole chain after the edit stops matching the copies everyone else holds."],
   ["Who adds the next block?","Nodes must agree on one version of history. Proof of Work (Bitcoin) makes miners spend electricity solving a puzzle; cheating costs more than it pays. Proof of Stake (Ethereum, Solana, Cardano) makes validators lock up coins as a bond that is destroyed if they misbehave. Both replace a trusted administrator with an economic penalty."],
   ["Why it matters","Because no single party controls the ledger, a blockchain can host money (Bitcoin), programmable contracts (Ethereum), and ownership records (NFTs, tokenised assets) that work the same for everyone, everywhere, 24/7. The cost is throughput: Bitcoin processes ~7 transactions per second on its base layer, Visa thousands. Layer-2 networks and newer Layer-1s narrow the gap — see our Top 7 Layer-1 and Layer-2 rankings."],
   ["What a blockchain is not","It is not anonymous (most are pseudonymous and fully traceable), not free (every transaction pays a fee), and not automatically trustworthy (the code is only as good as its authors and audits). Keep these three in mind and most scams become obvious."]],
  "faq":[["Is blockchain the same as Bitcoin?","No. Bitcoin is the first and largest application of a blockchain. Thousands of other blockchains exist with different rules and purposes."],["Can a blockchain be hacked?","The ledger itself is practically immutable on large networks. What gets hacked are exchanges, wallets, bridges and smart contracts built on top — which is why Step 4 and Step 7 are about security."],["Do I need to run a node?","No. Wallets talk to nodes for you. Running one is optional and improves your privacy and the network's resilience."]]},
 {"n":2,"slug":"step-2","title":"Bitcoin: Digital Gold Explained","read":"8 min","level":"Beginner",
  "desc":"Why Bitcoin has a fixed supply, how halving works, what miners do, and how to think about BTC as an asset.",
  "takeaways":["Bitcoin launched in January 2009; its creator, Satoshi Nakamoto, is anonymous and inactive.","Supply is capped at 21 million coins; about 94% have already been mined.","Every ~4 years the block reward halves (the 2024 halving cut it to 3.125 BTC).","Miners secure the network with electricity and are paid in new coins plus fees.","Spot Bitcoin ETFs (approved in the US in January 2024) let traditional accounts hold exposure.","BTC is volatile: 50–80% drawdowns have happened in every cycle.","The investment thesis is scarcity plus the strongest security of any network."],
  "sections":[
   ["Why 21 million","Bitcoin's issuance schedule is written into the code and enforced by every node. New coins enter only as block rewards, and those rewards halve on a fixed schedule until roughly the year 2140. No central bank can print more; changing the cap would require convincing the whole network to run new rules — something no group has ever achieved."],
   ["Mining in one paragraph","Miners race to find a number that makes the block hash fall below a target. The winner broadcasts the block and collects the reward. Difficulty adjusts every 2,016 blocks so blocks arrive about every 10 minutes no matter how many miners join. The energy spent is the cost of attacking the network: to rewrite history you would need more computing power than every honest miner combined."],
   ["The halving cycle","Rewards: 50 BTC (2009) → 25 (2012) → 12.5 (2016) → 6.25 (2020) → 3.125 (2024) → 1.5625 (expected 2028). Historically, the 12–18 months after each halving saw strong price appreciation as new supply shrank, though the sample size is small and past cycles are not a guarantee."],
   ["How people hold BTC","Four routes: an exchange account (easiest, custodial — see our Top 7 Exchanges), a self-custody wallet (you hold the keys — Top 7 Wallets), a spot ETF in a brokerage account (no keys, standard tax treatment), or a Bitcoin treasury company's stock (indirect, leveraged). Most long-term holders use a hardware wallet for the bulk and an exchange for buying."],
   ["Risks worth naming","Volatility, regulatory shifts, loss of keys, exchange failures (Mt. Gox 2014, FTX 2022), and the possibility that mining revenue from fees alone fails to secure the network decades from now. None of these are hidden; price them in."]],
  "faq":[["Can I buy less than one Bitcoin?","Yes. One BTC divides into 100 million satoshis; you can buy $10 worth on any exchange."],["Is Bitcoin legal?","Legal to own in most countries; a few restrict or ban it. Check local rules before buying."],["ETF or self-custody?","ETF for simplicity inside retirement accounts; self-custody if you want to actually own the asset and transact with it."]]},
 {"n":3,"slug":"step-3","title":"Ethereum & Smart Contracts","read":"8 min","level":"Beginner",
  "desc":"How Ethereum turned the blockchain into a world computer: smart contracts, gas, ETH staking and the Layer-2 roadmap.",
  "takeaways":["Ethereum (2015) added programmable contracts to the blockchain idea.","A smart contract is code that runs exactly as written, without an operator.","Gas is the fee paid to run computations; it rises with demand.","Ethereum moved to Proof of Stake in 2022, cutting energy use ~99.9%.","Stakers earn protocol yield; Lido and exchanges let you stake without 32 ETH.","Layer-2 rollups (Arbitrum, Base, Optimism) make Ethereum apps cheap.","Most DeFi, NFTs and stablecoin volume still settle on Ethereum."],
  "sections":[
   ["From ledger to computer","Bitcoin records who owns what. Ethereum adds a virtual machine (the EVM) so the ledger can also store programs and their state. Anyone can deploy a program — a smart contract — and anyone can call it. Lending markets, exchanges, games and stablecoins are all just contracts."],
   ["Smart contracts, honestly","\"Smart\" means automatic, not intelligent. A contract does exactly what its code says, forever, whether or not that's what the author intended. That makes audits essential and explains why DeFi hacks happen: the code had a bug and the blockchain faithfully executed it."],
   ["Gas and fees","Every operation costs gas, priced in gwei (billionths of ETH). Since EIP-1559, a base fee is burned and a tip goes to validators, so heavy usage can make ETH deflationary. When the base layer is busy, fees spike — which is why apps moved to Layer-2 rollups that batch thousands of transactions and post them to Ethereum for a fraction of the cost."],
   ["Staking","Validators lock 32 ETH to propose and attest to blocks and earn 3–4% annually. Liquid staking (Lido's stETH, Rocket Pool's rETH) and exchange staking let you participate with any amount. Our staking calculator shows what compounding does over years."],
   ["Where to go next","Try a Layer-2: withdraw a small amount from Coinbase to Base or from Binance to Arbitrum, swap on Uniswap, and watch the fee be a few cents. Then read the Top 7 DeFi list before putting real money to work."]],
  "faq":[["Ethereum vs Bitcoin — do I need both?","They solve different problems: BTC is a scarce monetary asset; ETH is the fuel and collateral of an app platform. Many portfolios hold both."],["What's an ERC-20 token?","A standard for fungible tokens on Ethereum. Stablecoins like USDC and most altcoins you see on exchanges are ERC-20s or equivalents on other chains."],["Is ETH supply capped?","No hard cap, but issuance is low and fee burning can make net supply shrink in busy periods."]]},
 {"n":4,"slug":"step-4","title":"Wallets & Self-Custody","read":"9 min","level":"Beginner",
  "desc":"Hot vs cold wallets, seed phrases, hardware devices and the seven rules that keep your crypto yours.",
  "takeaways":["A wallet stores keys, not coins; the coins live on the blockchain.","Your seed phrase (12–24 words) is the master key — anyone with it owns everything.","Hot wallets (MetaMask, Phantom) are convenient; cold wallets (Ledger, Trezor) are for savings.","Never type a seed phrase into a website or app that asks for it.","Use a hardware wallet for anything over a month's salary.","Verify addresses on the device screen, not just the computer.","Back up on steel, store in two places, tell one trusted person where."],
  "sections":[
   ["Keys, not coins","A wallet generates a private key, derives public addresses from it, and signs transactions. The blockchain only checks the signature. Lose the key and the coins are frozen forever; leak the key and they're gone in minutes. Custody is the whole game."],
   ["Hot vs cold","Hot wallets run on an internet-connected device — fast, free, exposed to malware and phishing. Cold (hardware) wallets keep the key in a sealed chip and sign offline; the computer only sees the signature. Our Top 7 Wallets list ranks both. The standard setup: a hardware wallet for savings plus a hot wallet with a small balance for daily use."],
   ["The seven security rules","1) Write the seed on paper or steel, never digitally. 2) Never share or type it anywhere except the wallet itself during recovery. 3) Buy hardware wallets only from the manufacturer. 4) Verify the receiving address on the device screen. 5) Send a small test amount first. 6) Revoke old token approvals (revoke.cash). 7) Assume every DM offering help is a scam."],
   ["Setting up a hardware wallet","Unbox, initialise on the device (never from a pre-filled card), write the seed, confirm it, set a PIN, install the companion app, and receive a $5 test transfer before moving real funds. Thirty minutes, once, protects you from the single most common way people lose crypto."],
   ["Inheritance and backups","Store one seed backup at home and one off-site (a safe-deposit box or a trusted relative). Leave sealed instructions with your will. Multi-signature setups or services like Casa add redundancy for large holdings."]],
  "faq":[["What if my hardware wallet breaks?","Buy any compatible device, enter your seed phrase, and your coins reappear. The device is replaceable; the seed is not."],["Is a phone wallet safe?","Fine for pocket-money amounts. Phones get stolen, SIM-swapped and infected; keep savings on hardware."],["Can the wallet company take my coins?","Not with self-custody — they never hold your key. Exchanges, by contrast, do hold your coins (custodial)."]]},
 {"n":5,"slug":"step-5","title":"Exchanges & Buying Safely","read":"8 min","level":"Beginner",
  "desc":"How to pick an exchange, pass KYC, place your first order, understand fees and spreads, and withdraw to your own wallet.",
  "takeaways":["Centralised exchanges (CEX) hold your coins; decentralised exchanges (DEX) don't.","KYC (ID verification) is standard on regulated exchanges and protects you from frozen funds later.","\"Simple buy\" buttons hide a 1–2% spread; the trading screen charges 0.1–0.6%.","Limit orders avoid overpaying in volatile markets.","Withdraw long-term holdings to self-custody; exchanges fail (FTX, Mt. Gox).","Enable 2FA with an app, never SMS; add a withdrawal whitelist.","Dollar-cost averaging beats trying to time the bottom for most people."],
  "sections":[
   ["Choosing an exchange","Weigh regulation in your country, fees, assets, and track record. Our Top 7 Exchanges shows a seven-metric score for each. Beginners: Coinbase (US) or Binance (elsewhere). Security-focused: Kraken. Derivatives: Bybit."],
   ["Your first purchase, step by step","Create account → verify ID → enable app-based 2FA → deposit via bank transfer (cheaper than card) → open the trading view, not the simple buy → place a limit order for BTC or ETH → confirm the fee shown. Expect a 0.1–0.6% fee instead of the 1–2% spread the \"Buy\" button charges."],
   ["Fees that actually matter","Three layers: trading fee (per order), spread (the gap between buy and sell price), and withdrawal fee (flat, per network). Use our fee comparison tool to see what 12 trades a month cost on each exchange. For withdrawals, choose a cheap network (e.g., USDC on Base or Solana rather than Ethereum L1)."],
   ["Withdraw to your wallet","After each purchase, send the coins to your hardware wallet (Step 4). Copy the address from your wallet, paste it in the exchange, select the matching network, send $5 first, confirm receipt, then send the rest. Keep a trading float on the exchange if you trade often."],
   ["Dollar-cost averaging","Buying a fixed amount every week removes the timing decision and emotional trades. Most exchanges automate it. Run the numbers in our DCA calculator before deciding the amount."]],
  "faq":[["Why does the exchange need my ID?","Anti-money-laundering law. Unregulated no-KYC venues are where frozen funds and exit scams cluster."],["Bank transfer or card?","Bank transfer: 0–1% fee. Card: 2–4%. Over a year of DCA the difference is real."],["Can I lose money if the exchange gets hacked?","Yes, unless it has insurance or proof-of-reserves and a solvency buffer. Minimise what you leave there."]]},
 {"n":6,"slug":"step-6","title":"DeFi, NFTs & Layer-2s","read":"9 min","level":"Intermediate",
  "desc":"Lend, swap and earn without intermediaries: how DeFi works, what NFTs really are, and why Layer-2 networks make it affordable.",
  "takeaways":["DeFi is finance run by smart contracts: lending (Aave), swapping (Uniswap), stablecoins (Sky), staking (Lido).","Yields come from borrowers, traders and protocol rewards — if you can't name the source, it's a red flag.","Stablecoins (USDC, USDT, DAI/USDS) are the on-chain dollar.","NFTs are unique tokens: ownership records for art, tickets, domains and game items.","Layer-2s cut fees from dollars to cents; start on Base or Arbitrum.","Bridging and token approvals are the two riskiest actions — do them carefully.","Smart-contract risk never hits zero: diversify and size positions."],
  "sections":[
   ["What DeFi replaces","A bank matches savers with borrowers, sets rates and takes a spread. Aave does the same with a smart contract: deposits earn, borrowers post collateral, rates adjust algorithmically, and liquidation is automatic. Uniswap replaces the exchange's order book with liquidity pools. Nobody can freeze you, and nobody will refund you either."],
   ["Where yield comes from","Lending interest (paid by borrowers), trading fees (paid by swappers to liquidity providers), staking rewards (paid by the protocol for securing it), and token incentives (paid by projects to attract users — temporary). Sustainable yields on major protocols are 2–10%. Anything advertising 50%+ is either incentives that will end or a scheme."],
   ["Stablecoins","USDC and USDT are backed by cash and treasuries held by companies; DAI/USDS is backed by on-chain collateral. They let you park value, earn yield and pay globally without volatility. Most DeFi positions start by converting to a stablecoin."],
   ["NFTs without the hype","A non-fungible token is a ledger entry that says this address owns item #1234 of collection X. The art market was the first use; tickets, domain names (.eth), game assets, music rights and real-world deeds are the lasting ones. Value depends entirely on what the issuer attaches to it."],
   ["Your first DeFi session","Withdraw $50 USDC from an exchange to Base. Install Rabby, connect to Aave, deposit USDC, watch it earn. Then swap $10 to ETH on Uniswap. Revoke the approvals afterwards at revoke.cash. Total fees: under a dollar. You now understand more than 95% of people talking about crypto."]],
  "faq":[["Is DeFi legal?","Using open protocols is legal in most places; some front-ends geo-block US users on certain features. Taxes apply to swaps and rewards."],["What is impermanent loss?","When you provide liquidity to a pool and prices diverge, you can end up with less value than simply holding. Fees may or may not cover it."],["What's a bridge?","A tool that moves assets between chains. Bridges have been the biggest hack targets — use official or audited ones and move only what you need."]]},
 {"n":7,"slug":"step-7","title":"Security, Taxes & the 7 Rules","read":"8 min","level":"Intermediate",
  "desc":"The scams that take the most money, how taxes work on crypto, and the seven rules that separate survivors from victims.",
  "takeaways":["Most losses are not hacks — they're phishing, fake support, romance/pig-butchering scams and seed-phrase theft.","Nobody legitimate will ever ask for your seed phrase or remote access.","Every sale, swap, and spend is usually a taxable event; staking rewards are income.","Use tax software from day one; reconstructing years later is painful.","Diversify custody: hardware wallet, one or two reputable exchanges.","Position sizing: never more than you can watch drop 70% without panic-selling.","Keep learning from primary sources: protocol docs, audits, on-chain data."],
  "sections":[
   ["The scam leaderboard","1) Pig-butchering: a stranger builds a relationship, then a 'guaranteed' trading platform. 2) Fake support: a DM after you post a question. 3) Address poisoning: a look-alike address in your history. 4) Wallet drainers: a site asks you to 'verify' or 'claim' and you sign a malicious approval. 5) Fake hardware wallets with pre-set seeds. 6) Impersonation giveaways. 7) Fake exchanges and apps. Recognise the shape and you'll dodge all of them."],
   ["Taxes in one screen","Buying with fiat: not taxable. Selling, swapping coin-to-coin, or spending: capital gain or loss on the difference from your cost basis. Staking, lending interest, airdrops, mining: ordinary income at receipt. Transfers between your own wallets: not taxable. Rules vary by country — the Top 7 Tax Tools handle the details."],
   ["Operational security","Separate email for crypto; app-based 2FA or a hardware key; a dedicated browser profile for DeFi; a hardware wallet for savings; a 'burner' hot wallet for minting and new apps; bookmarks instead of search results for exchange logins; and a monthly review of approvals."],
   ["The 7 rules","1) Only invest what you can lose. 2) Self-custody your savings. 3) Verify every address on the device. 4) Never share a seed. 5) Know where the yield comes from. 6) Log every transaction for tax. 7) Ignore urgency — scams and bad trades both run on it."],
   ["You've finished the path","Take the 7-question quiz to lock it in, get your Starter Kit, and subscribe to the 7-in-7 Digest so your knowledge keeps pace with the market."]],
  "faq":[["I was scammed — what now?","Move remaining funds to a new wallet immediately, revoke approvals, report to your exchange and local police/cyber-crime unit, and ignore anyone offering 'recovery' — that's the second scam."],["Do I owe tax if I never cashed out to fiat?","In most countries, yes: swapping one coin for another is a disposal."],["How often should I review security?","Monthly: approvals, 2FA devices, exchange whitelist, and firmware updates."]]}
]

GLOSSARY = [
 ("Address","A public identifier (like an account number) derived from a public key, used to receive crypto."),("Airdrop","Free distribution of tokens to wallets, often to reward early users or market a project."),("Altcoin","Any cryptocurrency other than Bitcoin."),("AMM","Automated Market Maker — a DEX model where prices are set by a formula over liquidity pools (Uniswap, Curve)."),("APY","Annual Percentage Yield — return per year including compounding."),("ASIC","Specialised mining hardware built for one hashing algorithm."),("Bear market","A prolonged period of falling prices and pessimism."),("Bitcoin","The first cryptocurrency (2009); a Proof-of-Work blockchain with a 21M supply cap."),("Block","A batch of transactions added to the chain, linked to the previous block by its hash."),("Block explorer","A website for reading blockchain data (Etherscan, Mempool.space, Solscan)."),("Blockchain","A distributed ledger of blocks linked by cryptographic hashes and maintained by consensus."),("Bridge","A protocol that moves assets between blockchains; a frequent hack target."),("Bull market","A prolonged period of rising prices and optimism."),("Burn","Permanently removing tokens from circulation by sending them to an unspendable address."),("CEX","Centralised exchange — a company that custodies your coins and matches trades (Coinbase, Binance)."),("Cold wallet","A wallet whose keys never touch an internet-connected device (hardware wallet, paper)."),("Consensus","The mechanism by which nodes agree on the ledger's state (Proof of Work, Proof of Stake)."),("Cost basis","What you paid for an asset, used to calculate taxable gains."),("Custodial","A service that holds your keys on your behalf (exchanges). Opposite: self-custody."),("DAO","Decentralised Autonomous Organisation — a group governed by token votes and smart contracts."),("DApp","Decentralised application — software whose backend runs on smart contracts."),("DCA","Dollar-cost averaging — buying a fixed amount at regular intervals."),("DeFi","Decentralised finance — lending, trading and other financial services run by smart contracts."),("DEX","Decentralised exchange — trading directly from your wallet via smart contracts (Uniswap, Jupiter)."),("Difficulty","How hard it is to mine a block; adjusts to keep block times stable."),("ERC-20","The Ethereum standard for fungible tokens."),("ERC-721","The Ethereum standard for NFTs."),("ETF","Exchange-traded fund — a stock-market product that holds an asset (spot BTC/ETH ETFs)."),("Ethereum","A smart-contract blockchain (2015); the largest DeFi and NFT ecosystem."),("EVM","Ethereum Virtual Machine — the runtime for smart contracts, also used by many other chains."),("Fiat","Government-issued currency (USD, EUR, INR)."),("Finality","The point after which a transaction cannot be reversed."),("FOMO","Fear of missing out — emotional buying during rallies."),("Fork","A change to protocol rules; a hard fork can split a chain (Bitcoin/Bitcoin Cash)."),("Gas","The fee paid to execute transactions or contracts on Ethereum-style chains."),("Gwei","One billionth of an ETH; the unit gas prices are quoted in."),("Halving","The scheduled 50% cut to Bitcoin's block reward every ~210,000 blocks (~4 years)."),("Hash","A fixed-length fingerprint of data; tiny input changes produce completely different hashes."),("Hash rate","Total computing power securing a Proof-of-Work network."),("HODL","Slang for holding long term regardless of volatility."),("Hot wallet","A wallet on an internet-connected device (browser extension, mobile app)."),("Impermanent loss","The shortfall a liquidity provider suffers versus simply holding when prices diverge."),("KYC","Know Your Customer — identity verification required by regulated exchanges."),("Layer 1","A base blockchain that settles its own transactions (Bitcoin, Ethereum, Solana)."),("Layer 2","A network that processes transactions off-chain and posts proofs/data to a Layer 1 (Arbitrum, Base)."),("Liquid staking","Staking via a protocol that issues a tradable receipt token (stETH)."),("Liquidation","Forced sale of collateral when a loan's health factor falls below the threshold."),("Liquidity","How easily an asset can be traded without moving the price."),("Liquidity pool","Tokens locked in a smart contract to enable trading on an AMM."),("Market cap","Price multiplied by circulating supply."),("Mainnet","The live production network, as opposed to a testnet."),("Mempool","The waiting room of unconfirmed transactions."),("Mining","Adding blocks to a Proof-of-Work chain by solving a hash puzzle for a reward."),("Multisig","A wallet requiring multiple signatures to spend (e.g., 2 of 3 keys)."),("NFT","Non-fungible token — a unique token representing ownership of a specific item."),("Node","A computer running blockchain software that validates and relays transactions."),("Nonce","A number used once; in mining, the value changed to search for a valid hash."),("Oracle","A service that feeds off-chain data (prices) to smart contracts (Chainlink)."),("Perpetuals","Derivatives contracts with no expiry, used for leveraged trading."),("Phishing","Tricking a user into revealing keys or signing malicious transactions via fake sites or messages."),("Private key","The secret number that signs transactions; whoever holds it controls the funds."),("Proof of Reserves","An audit showing an exchange holds the assets it owes customers."),("Proof of Stake","Consensus where validators lock coins as collateral; misbehaviour is penalised (slashing)."),("Proof of Work","Consensus where miners expend energy to add blocks; attacking costs more than it pays."),("Public key","Derived from the private key; used to generate addresses and verify signatures."),("Rollup","A Layer-2 that bundles many transactions and posts compressed data or proofs to Layer 1."),("Rug pull","A scam where developers abandon a project after taking investor funds."),("Satoshi","The smallest unit of Bitcoin (0.00000001 BTC); also Bitcoin's pseudonymous creator."),("Seed phrase","12–24 words that regenerate all keys in a wallet; the ultimate backup and the ultimate risk."),("Sequencer","The entity that orders transactions on a rollup; usually centralised today."),("Sharding","Splitting a blockchain's data or execution into parallel pieces to scale."),("Slashing","Destroying part of a validator's stake as a penalty for misbehaviour."),("Slippage","The difference between expected and executed trade price."),("Smart contract","Code deployed on a blockchain that executes automatically when called."),("Solana","A high-throughput Proof-of-Stake blockchain known for low fees and consumer apps."),("Stablecoin","A token pegged to a fiat currency (USDC, USDT, DAI/USDS)."),("Staking","Locking coins to help secure a Proof-of-Stake network in exchange for rewards."),("Testnet","A sandbox network with valueless coins for development."),("Token","A digital asset issued on a blockchain, distinct from the chain's native coin."),("Tokenomics","A token's supply, issuance, distribution and utility design."),("TPS","Transactions per second — a throughput measure."),("TVL","Total value locked — assets deposited in a DeFi protocol."),("Two-factor authentication (2FA)","A second login factor; use an authenticator app or hardware key, not SMS."),("Validator","A Proof-of-Stake participant that proposes and attests to blocks."),("Wallet","Software or hardware that manages keys and signs transactions."),("Web3","The umbrella term for blockchain-based, user-owned internet applications."),("Whale","An entity holding enough of an asset to move its price."),("Whitepaper","A document describing a project's design and purpose; Bitcoin's is nine pages."),("Yield farming","Moving capital between DeFi protocols to maximise rewards."),("ZK proof","Zero-knowledge proof — cryptographic proof that a statement is true without revealing the data; powers ZK rollups.")
]

